{"causes": ["The decline of the central Ottoman authority in the 18th century allowed Mamluk governors in Baghdad to establish de facto autonomous rule.", "Internal power struggles among Mamluk factions in Baghdad following the death of Sa'id Pasha created an opportunity for Davud Pasha to ascend to power in 1816.", "Sultan Mahmud II's centralizing Tanzimat reforms in the early 19th century directly challenged the semi-autonomous status of Mamluk Iraq, setting the stage for conflict."], "effects": ["Davud Pasha's defeat in 1831 marked the definitive end of Mamluk rule in Iraq after nearly 80 years of semi-independence.", "His removal led to the re-establishment of direct Ottoman control over Baghdad and the full integration of Iraq into the imperial administrative system.", "The collapse of the Mamluk order in Iraq facilitated the implementation of Tanzimat reforms, leading to significant administrative and military restructuring in the province."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "mahmud-ii", "sourceName": "Mahmud II", "verb": "CAUSES", "targetSlug": "kölemen-davut-paşa", "targetName": "Kölemen Davut Paşa", "context": "Sultan Mahmud II's centralizing policies and Tanzimat reforms directly led to the Ottoman military campaign against Davud Pasha in 1831."}, {"sourceSlug": "kölemen-davut-paşa", "sourceName": "Kölemen Davut Paşa", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "ottoman-mamluk-war-1831", "targetName": "Ottoman-Mamluk War of 1831", "context": "Davud Pasha was the principal Mamluk leader defending Baghdad during the Ottoman siege in 1831 that ended Mamluk rule in Iraq."}, {"sourceSlug": "kölemen-davut-paşa", "sourceName": "Kölemen Davut Paşa", "verb": "CREATES", "targetSlug": "iraq-first-printing-press", "targetName": "Iraq's First Printing Press", "context": "Davud Pasha established Iraq's first printing press in Baghdad in 1830 as part of his modernization efforts."}, {"sourceSlug": "siege-of-baghdad-1831", "sourceName": "Siege of Baghdad (1831)", "verb": "